  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n- Areas of degraded air quality from regional wildfire smoke. \n\n- Warming trend through Friday this week. \n\n- Elevated fire weather concerns by Friday, from hot, dry, and \n  breezy conditions. \n\nSatellite imagery this afternoon shows widespread smoke impacting\nthe Northern Rockies associated with wildfire activity. This \nsmoke has reduced visibility and has prompted to the issuance of \nvarious air quality alerts from the Idaho and Montana DEQ offices.\nSurface observations highlight a cold front tracking along and \neast of the divide. Looking ahead to this evening and overnight, a\npressure gradient will strengthen along the divide, with easterly\nwinds being felt as far west as the US-93 corridor from Kalispell\nthrough the Bitterroot Valley. The strongest winds overnight will\nfocus over the higher terrain, with gusts of 20-25 mph. \n\nConfidence is high for a warming trend through Friday this week. \nGlobal ensembles show a large ridge of high pressure slowly \nshifting eastward from the Pacific Northwest over the Northern \nRockies by Thursday into Friday. A hot, dry and warm airmass will \nsettle across the region during this time, increasing fire weather\nconcerns. We are closely monitoring Friday for potential critical\nfire weather as model guidance suggests an upper level wave will \nmove across the ridge, with increasing winds by Friday afternoon \nand evening. The highest probabilities (40-60%) for gusts to 30 \nmph or higher are focused along and east of the divide. \n\nLooking ahead to next weekend, global ensembles and their \nclusters suggest the ridge of high pressure will become centered \nacross the Four Corners region, with a dry westerly flow pattern \nacross the Northern Rockies. This pattern suggests seasonably warm\ntemperatures, with little to any chances for precipitation.",
